<node id="NFC">
  <data key="d0">NFC</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NFC (Near Field Communication) is a short-range wireless technology used for device-to-device interactions, and can be toggled on via the control centre.&lt;SEP&gt;NFC (Near Field Communication) is a technology that was not supported by Watch Pro.&lt;SEP&gt;NFC is a communication technology embedded in the Nothing Phone, allowing for wireless data transfer and device pairing features.&lt;SEP&gt;NFC is a short-range wireless technology that can be enabled on the Nothing Phone through the settings or the control center.&lt;SEP&gt;NFC is a short-range wireless technology that enables the Nothing Phone to interact with nearby devices and services, which can be enabled through the Connection preferences section.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9dbf1c133d4ea12599adde2c8babfb03&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1d82ee9e80b9a4011cc5dfdad909fc2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-281d19d9c4f252fd5c844c3d467ca398&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6dc70b538423eed2a153e23decae4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43c5f3a3b39a02f332e40eef921a1f87</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1767407143</data>
</node>
<node id="eSIM">
  <data key="d0">eSIM</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">eSIM is a digital SIM card that can be used on the Nothing Phone in specific versions sold in Japan.&lt;SEP&gt;eSIM is a digital SIM that allows a phone to support a cellular network without a physical SIM card, but Nothing Phone does not support this.&lt;SEP&gt;eSIM is a feature that Watch Pro does not support, limiting its connectivity options.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-258395120725348800696992422fd798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6dc70b538423eed2a153e23decae4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43c5f3a3b39a02f332e40eef921a1f87</data>
