</node>
<node id="Flip to Glyph">
  <data key="d0">Flip to Glyph</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Flip to Glyph is a feature allowing users to trigger silent mode and Glyph Light-only alerts by placing the phone in a specific orientation, enhancing focus time and notification management.&lt;SEP&gt;Flip to Glyph is a feature that activates silent mode and Glyph Light-only alerts when the phone is placed face up on a flat surface.&lt;SEP&gt;Flip to Glyph is a feature that activates silent mode and uses Glyph lighting for notifications when the phone is placed face-up on a flat surface.&lt;SEP&gt;Flip to Glyph is a feature that activates silent mode and uses visual alerts through the Glyph Interface when the phone is placed on a flat surface with that side up.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77d37b7781a21f9ace596c3bcb0cbfd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b7b5b1eb951ce536bdbb599b623332a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9ca0a66c298c31fec04cbfc30d7b98c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9025d462a4f9ed5f5840d5850b11449</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1767407143</data>
</node>
<node id="Bedtime Schedule">
  <data key="d0">Bedtime Schedule</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Bedtime Schedule is a feature under the Glyph Interface that lets users temporarily disable the interface at set times and days to avoid disturbances.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bedtime Schedule is a function that temporarily disables the Glyph Interface during specified times and days to prevent nighttime disturbances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-281d19d9c4f252fd5c844c3d467ca398&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9025d462a4f9ed5f5840d5850b11449</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1767407143</data>
</node>
<node id="Data Roaming">
  <data key="d0">Data Roaming</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Data Roaming refers to the use of data services on a network outside of the user's home region, which typically incurs higher fees than home network usage. It requires enabling the data-roaming feature in the phone's settings beforehand to ensure connectivity while abroad. This feature allows a phone to access data services on foreign networks, often resulting in increased costs due to the nature of international data plans. Activation of data roaming is necessary to avoid unexpected charges, as it is not enabled by default on most devices.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77d37b7781a21f9ace596c3bcb0cbfd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b7b5b1eb951ce536bdbb599b623332a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d9ff89b42c8123f60ebe4521bfb0cbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0028e301a1da668320afc0e2e78601b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcde7317fc7b036415d89ea81329b232&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9025d462a4f9ed5f5840d5850b11449</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1767407143</data>
</node>
<node id="OTG Connection">
  <data key="d0">OTG Connection</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">OTG Connection is a standard that allows mobile devices to connect directly with each other, facilitating data transfer and charging.&lt;SEP&gt;OTG connection refers to a USB standard that enables mobile devices to connect directly with other devices, bypassing the need for a computer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fcde7317fc7b036415d89ea81329b232&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9025d462a4f9ed5f5840d5850b11449</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1767407143</data>
</node>
<node id="Charging Cable">
  <data key="d0">Charging Cable</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Charging Cable is a physical component included in the box for Phone (2), with a length of approximately 1 meter.&lt;SEP&gt;Charging Cable refers to the included Type-C cable for charging the Phone, with a length of about 1 meter.&lt;SEP&gt;Charging cable is a physical cable used to charge the Nothing Phone, with a length of approximately 1 meter.&lt;SEP&gt;The Charging Cable is a Type-C cable included in the box for Phone (2a), used for charging the device.&lt;SEP&gt;The Charging Cable is a standard USB-C cable included in the box for Nothing Phone (1), used for wired charging.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77d37b7781a21f9ace596c3bcb0cbfd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9025d462a4f9ed5f5840d5850b11449&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b7b5b1eb951ce536bdbb599b623332a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-281d19d9c4f252fd5c844c3d467ca398&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41ebb68d062de192afe8e06b1a67a1c4</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1767407143</data>
</node>
<node id="USB On The Go">
  <data key="d0">USB On The Go</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">USB On The Go (OTG) is a standard that enables the Nothing Phone to connect directly with other devices for data transfer and charging.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9025d462a4f9ed5f5840d5850b11449</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1767407143</data>
</node>
<node id="Bluetooth Tethering">
  <data key="d0">Bluetooth Tethering</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Bluetooth Tethering is a method of sharing a phone's internet connection via Bluetooth, enabling other devices to access the internet through the phone's data connection. It is used for connecting other devices to the phone's network, allowing the Nothing Phone to connect to other devices for network access. This process involves sharing internet connectivity from the Nothing Phone to another device through a Bluetooth connection, and it requires both the phone and the other device to be connected and configured. Bluetooth tethering is a feature that enables sharing the phone's internet connection with a connected device through a Bluetooth connection, ensuring both devices have network access to facilitate the transfer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cecbf41a5540f78302f91235b833c7dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b7b5b1eb951ce536bdbb599b623332a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea4524a05ac17de1be9923e9a9dc9c59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41ebb68d062de192afe8e06b1a67a1c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9ca0a66c298c31fec04cbfc30d7b98c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-281d19d9c4f252fd5c844c3d467ca398&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9025d462a4f9ed5f5840d5850b11449</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1767407148</data>
</node>
<node id="Status Bar">
  <data key="d0">Status Bar</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Status Bar is the top portion of the phone's display screen that provides visual information such as time, battery percentage, and network connectivity status. It is a display element located at the top of the Nothing Phone screen, showing system information including time, battery percentage, and network connection status. The Status Bar can be accessed through swipe gestures or quick settings, allowing users to view essential system data and controls. It serves as a central area for displaying icons related to system functions, such as battery level, time, and network connectivity, ensuring users have quick access to important information.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77d37b7781a21f9ace596c3bcb0cbfd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b7b5b1eb951ce536bdbb599b623332a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d9ff89b42c8123f60ebe4521bfb0cbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0028e301a1da668320afc0e2e78601b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcde7317fc7b036415d89ea81329b232&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9025d462a4f9ed5f5840d5850b11449</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1767407152</data>
</node>
<node id="Mobile Devices">
  <data key="d0">Mobile Devices</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mobile devices are the entities that can connect via USB OTG, and they traditionally required a computer to connect to a network or other devices.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-258395120725348800696992422fd798</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1767407152</data>
