</edge>
<edge source="Active Noise Cancellation" target="Dual Connection">
  <data key="d6">7.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Dual Connection functionality may affect the operation of Active Noise Cancellation, depending on audio activity and connection priorities.</data>
  <data key="d8">audio prioritization,feature interaction</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-6e908dc520371478d52528beaf43780b</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407197</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Active Noise Cancellation" target="Smart ANC">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Smart ANC is a specific implementation of Active Noise Cancellation that dynamically adapts to environmental noise levels.</data>
  <data key="d8">adaptive technology,feature refinement</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-cbeb4732200e7559b14badeb7c65f47c</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407197</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Active Noise Cancellation" target="Adaptive Mode">
  <data key="d6">7.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Adaptive Mode is a subset of Active Noise Cancellation features that adjust the level of noise cancellation based on environmental conditions and user behavior.</data>
  <data key="d8">environmental adaptation,noise control</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-cbeb4732200e7559b14badeb7c65f47c</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407197</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Dual Connection" target="Continuous Music Playback Time">
  <data key="d6">6.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Dual Connection can impact Continuous Music Playback Time by utilizing bandwidth and affecting audio quality.</data>
  <data key="d8">bandwidth usage,performance effects</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-6e908dc520371478d52528beaf43780b</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407197</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Dual Connection" target="Call audio">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Call audio is prioritized in Dual Connection, meaning if a call is active on one device, Nothing Ear will direct the call audio to that device or switch as per rules.</data>
  <data key="d8">audio routing,call priority</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-8c10ba86f3c652add8c1d23db1a9625b</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407197</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Dual Connection" target="Audio output">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Audio output on Nothing Ear is determined by Dual Connection rules, which depend on the state of audio on connected devices and which device is most recently active.</data>
  <data key="d8">audio routing,connection logic</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-8c10ba86f3c652add8c1d23db1a9625b</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407197</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Dual Connection" target="Bluetooth Fast Pair">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Bluetooth Fast Pair and Dual Connection are related connectivity features that may have compatibility issues affecting sound output.</data>
  <data key="d8">device connectivity,technological features</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-a1887c3e753e5f27cc1105a32465893f</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407207</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Dual Connection" target="sound output">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The use of Dual Connection may lead to sound output delays when trying to switch music channels or connect devices.</data>
  <data key="d8">audio performance,connectivity issues</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-a1887c3e753e5f27cc1105a32465893f</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407207</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LDAC" target="LHDC">
  <data key="d6">6.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Both LHDC and LDAC are high-resolution audio codecs, but they differ in transmission speed and compression levels, with LHDC offering higher bit rates and less compression than LDAC.</data>
  <data key="d8">audio technology,codec comparison</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-026c9aac607d8ab10d1f5ae9d51556e9</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407197</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Nothing Tech" target="QR Code">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">"The QR Code provided by Nothing Tech directs users to the official website for downloading the Nothing X app.</data>
  <data key="d8">product access,user guidance</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-9ca8764057d5d820c4c3fb502a440d2f</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407152</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iOS" target="Buds Pro">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The Buds Pro earbuds are compatible with iOS devices, enabling interaction through touch gestures and app controls.</data>
  <data key="d8">device compatibility,platform integration</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-3c721c853ee8f8f0dc302ae4d23208b8</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407152</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iOS" target="Buds">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The Buds are compatible with iOS devices running version 13 and above.</data>
  <data key="d8">device support,operating system compatibility</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-8464f2a22c355940190a72202a4bd5a3</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407152</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iOS" target="ENC">
  <data key="d6">7.0</data>
  <data key="d7">iOS devices only support the AAC Bluetooth codec, which means they cannot utilize features like LDAC or LHDC, but they can still use the ENC feature for noise cancellation during calls.</data>
  <data key="d8">noise cancellation,operating system limitations</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-026c9aac607d8ab10d1f5ae9d51556e9</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407197</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iOS" target="Google Fast Pair">
  <data key="d6">7.0</data>
  <data key="d7">iOS devices do not support Google Fast Pair, but they can still use the Ear (a) earbuds with the AAC codec, which is the only supported format on iOS.</data>
  <data key="d8">codec support,operating system limitations</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-026c9aac607d8ab10d1f5ae9d51556e9</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407197</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Reconnection" target="Connection Priority">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Reconnection is governed by the Connection Priority rules, which determine which device the earbuds reconnect to based on the activity state of connected devices.</data>
  <data key="d8">connection rules,reconnection logic</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-f8b23107caad65d85305805a37ddbe28</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407152</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Connection Priority" target="Music State">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Connection Priority is influenced by the Music State, where the device that is playing audio takes priority in maintaining the connection.</data>
  <data key="d8">connection logic,device activity</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-f8b23107caad65d85305805a37ddbe28</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407152</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Connection Priority" target="Call State">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Connection Priority is affected by the Call State, as active calls on a device disable reconnection switching and prevent connection changes.</data>
  <data key="d8">call state,connection rules</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-f8b23107caad65d85305805a37ddbe28</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407152</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Call State" target="Dual Connectivity">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Call State interacts with Dual Connectivity in determining the behavior of the Neckband Pro, as it can prevent or modify connection switches during active calls.</data>
  <data key="d8">connection prioritization,device state interaction</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-cbad053247bfe41ce192c651034259c3</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407152</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Head-Related Transfer Function (HRTF)" target="Spatial Audio Effect">
  <data key="d6">18.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Spatial Audio Effect is based on the Head-Related Transfer Function (HRTF) model for creating 3D audio.&lt;SEP&gt;The Spatial Audio Effect relies on the Head-Related Transfer Function (HRTF) model to create a 3D audio experience, making HRTF a foundational component of this feature.</data>
  <data key="d8">audio modeling,audio simulation,immersive sound</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-310bcdfd35de1b898e48e8659f40d35e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93607977c7e870361284500bad913d36</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407152</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Spatial Audio Effect" target="ANC">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">ANC and the Spatial Audio Effect are both audio enhancement features of the CMF Buds 2, contributing to the overall audio experience.</data>
  <data key="d8">audio features,sound optimization</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-3a8564fc99ee9391246494d993bc4be4</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1767407152</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Spatial Audio Effect" target="Neckband Pro">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The Neckband Pro uses the Spatial Audio Effect, which provides a 3D immersive listening experience based on the HRTF model, offering cinematic-grade audio quality.</data>
  <data key="d8">audio experience,product feature</data>
